A political parties submod for [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3737705389]Flavorium Universalis[/url].

[b]Requires Flavorium Universalis.[/b]

[hr][/hr]

[i]Early Alpha preview. This submod has not been played through yet, and its numbers are first guesses.[/i]

[h2]Four parties out of eleven[/h2]

Your realm has four political parties, chosen from eleven by your government, the age and the strength of your estates: the Court Party, the Aristocratic Faction, the Merchant Interest, the Clerical Party, the War Party, the Agrarian Party, the Reform Party, the Absolutist Party, the Regional Party, the Colonial Lobby and the Liberal Party. The line-up is reviewed every fifteen to twenty-five years, and parties already in place get a head start.

Some realms get the names their parties actually had: Hooks and Cods in Holland, Guelphs and Ghibellines in early Italy, Hats and Caps in Sweden, Tories and Whigs in Britain.

[h2]Every minister belongs to one[/h2]

A minister joins a party when seated: their cabinet traits decide it first, then their estate. Each minister brings their party 2 appointed seats in the chamber, and the party's most senior minister leads it. Its mood follows its members into office: ministers of a pleased party work 5% better, ministers of an angry one 20% worse.

[h2]The chamber[/h2]

Seats come from the realm: every megalopolis is worth 3, every city 2, every town 1 and every rural settlement a fifth of one, and each location's seats are shared among the estates by their power there. The chamber grows with the realm from 20 to 84 seats, so seats are always a share. A party's power is its share of the chamber, shown seat by seat in the Factions panel.

[h2]Satisfaction, agendas and the cabinet[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Satisfaction:[/b] reviewed silently every two to four years, from how the party's estate is treated and whether it has seats in the cabinet. Pleased parties give the realm a bonus and angry ones a penalty, scaled by their power.
[*][b]Agendas:[/b] a party with a quarter of the power sets one every five to twelve years and gives you six years. Met: +30 satisfaction. Missed: -25.
[*][b]Stacked or balanced:[/b] a cabinet run by one party is efficient but breeds rivalry. A balanced cabinet is steadier and cheaper to manage.
[*][b]Leading party:[/b] the strongest party moves society its way.
[/list]

[h2]Five actions and an election[/h2]

Select a party's card to act on it, mostly for political influence: bribe it, attack it, ask it a favour, appoint its leader to the cabinet, or concede its agenda by decree. Calling an election recounts the seats and reviews the parties at once.

[h2]Parties you cannot ignore[/h2]

The War Party, the Regional Party and the Liberal Party escalate if left angry and powerful: a warning, then unrest, then a coup, a provincial rising or a liberal uprising. Placate them, or attack them below a fifth of the seats.

From the Age of Absolutism, Liberal Thought spreads through your cities. When it is widespread enough, the Liberal Party forms, and while reformers or liberals lead the realm, ministers serve ten-year terms.

[h2]Quiet by design[/h2]

Satisfaction and agenda results appear only in the panel. Announcements share a budget of about two events a decade, and the one character event fires only for ministers currently in the cabinet. Parties are simulated for you, the great powers and realms with 100 or more development. A game rule turns the system off.
